{
  "WHAT_THIS_IS": "Arm matching and gradient direction, on the REAL step-199 model.\n\nWHY THIS EXISTS. Every arm-matching number this study has was measured on\nfully-trained public GPT-2. `docs/measurements/8b-i-in-context-match.md` says so\nin its own limitations:\n\n    These numbers come from fully-trained public GPT-2. The burst is injected\n    into a from-scratch model at step 200 ... Arms matched here are NOT\n    guaranteed to be matched on that model, and that model is the one whose\n    weights actually move. This is a PROXY until it can be re-verified against\n    a real step-200 checkpoint.\n\n`8b-iv`'s LIMITATION says the same thing harder (\"THAT MODEL DOES NOT EXIST\",\n\"PROXY OF UNKNOWN FIDELITY\"). That model now exists:\n`runs-fixed/seed00_twin/step000199_weights_only.pt`, trained at `3e715a6`, and\nbit-identical to the injecting arm's checkpoint at the same step. This is the\nre-verification those two documents ask for.\n\nDEFINITIONS ARE INHERITED, NOT REINVENTED. The sequences come from\n`injection.build_plan`, so they are the sequences the runs actually inject --\nnot a re-assembly that happens to agree. The single-sequence statistics use\n`burst_match.per_token_losses` / `flat_gradient` and `gradient_direction`'s\nregion-loss definition, so the numbers are comparable to 8b-i and 8b-iv rather\nthan merely similar in spirit.\n\nTHREE MATCHING TARGETS, AND THE SPEC HAS NOT PICKED ONE. `docs/spec-v4.md`\n\"Deliberately still open\" item 3 lists them: burst alone, burst in a 1024-token\nsequence, or full-batch delta. All three are reported and NONE is recommended.\nItem 1 -- the tolerance -- is not set, and nothing here applies one. Item 4 --\nthe definition of \"anisotropy\" -- is open, so the two statistics that bear on it\nare reported under their own names and the umbrella word is not used.\n\nTHE FULL-BATCH DELTA HAS NEVER BEEN COMPUTED, by this script's predecessors or\nanyone. It is the one that says what the burst does to the update that actually\nlands: the injected sequence is 1 row of 256, so a difference that is large at\nsequence level can be diluted 256x by the time it reaches the optimizer. It is\ncomputed here the way `train.py` computes it -- 32 micro-batches of 8, each\nloss divided by `accum` before `backward`, under bf16 autocast -- because a\ngradient assembled any other way is a different number (S77).\n\nNO VERDICT. Nothing here says whether the arms are matched. That is the\ntolerance decision, and it belongs to whoever runs the study.",
